@nikdon Updating to akka 2.4.9 have introduced a regression on my test suite in regards to the checkSameOrigin. 
In fact in akka 2.4.8 on a request with an invalid origin the response was reporting the invalid origin, something like `Invalid `Origin` header values: http://invalidorigin.com` where as now the response contains the allowed origins `Allowed `Origin` header values: http://allowedorigin.com` .

I was wondering why this change of semantics in the checkSameOrigin directive. Wouldn't be better, also in term of security, to not expose unnecessary information and therefore keeping the semantic of checkSameOrigin as in 2.4.8 ?

This was not what I intended to test, it should have been `Msg2.class` here and without the `handleMsg(Msg2 msg)` method.

However, that also works.

```java
  private void handleMsg(Msg msg) {
    result("match Msg");
  }

  private void handleMsg(Msg1 msg) {
    result("match Msg1");
  }

  @Test
  public void shouldMatchDelegatingToGeneralMethod() {
    Receive rcv = ReceiveBuilder.create()
        .match(Msg1.class, this::handleMsg)
        .match(Msg2.class, this::handleMsg)
        .build();
    assertTrue(rcv.onMessage().isDefinedAt(new Msg1()));
    rcv.onMessage().apply(new Msg1());
    assertEquals("match Msg1", result());

    assertTrue(rcv.onMessage().isDefinedAt(new Msg2("foo")));
    rcv.onMessage().apply(new Msg2("foo"));
    assertEquals("match Msg", result()); // note that this doesn't go to "match Msg2"
  }
```

